From 03eba1107fe4133fabfa2895fc7de06e81e04fe5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilan Joselevich <personal@ilanjoselevich.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 19:31:36 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] daemon: read a build log from the log store or the
 binary cache

A builder that left the farm takes Nix's kept logs with it, and a node
asked about a build it never ran has nothing to send. After LIVE and
KEPT, GetBuildLog now asks the farm's log store (--build-log-store),
then its binary cache (--niks3-cache-url).

The log store's copy is read whole with getBuildLog and served as
STORE. niks3 already uploads the log of every built output it pushes as
log/<drv>, so the node fetches <url>/log/<drv> through Nix's file
transfer, which applies its netrc and decodes niks3's zstd, and serves
it as CACHE. Both go through the decode cache, so offsets, limits and
tails work on them, and they share its two decode slots. Uploads are at
most 64 MiB, so a node holds at most 128 MiB of store reads in memory.
At shutdown the daemon destroys the decode cache before the log store.

A store or cache that cannot be read, through an outage or expired
credentials, is passed over, so the other can still answer. When
neither has the log, an unreadable one answers UNAVAILABLE rather than
NOT_FOUND or DATA_LOSS. HTTP 404, and the 403 S3 sends for a key it
does not have, read as NOT_FOUND. Messages name a URL without its user,
password or query, since they go to watchers. The cache holds built
logs only, until niks3's next garbage collection.

Helm passes niks3.cacheURL to the builders too, and the buildLogStore
option says that nodes read from the store.

log-cache-test covers a missing log and one that stops partway, from a
source that can and cannot be read. In checks.vm, one test server plays
the binary cache and the log stores. The mTLS daemon reads a zstd log
through a cache URL with credentials and a query, from the start and
from an offset, and gets NOT_FOUND for 403 and UNAVAILABLE for 401 with
no credentials in the message. The module's daemon answers from its log
store once the kept file is gone. A daemon whose store answers 401
falls back to the cache, and answers UNAVAILABLE when the cache has
nothing either. In checks.farm, the builder that did not run a build
may answer with the cache's copy of its log.
